{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790269354,"nonce":"bHJkOJI8SSSTyklNHQjUlKbsWzw=","title":"Python: a generator expression passed where other callers pass lists arrives as None","message":"A parameter whose callers pass both a list (or range) and a generator\nexpression reads the generator as None, or as an empty list.\n\n    def ls(x):\n        return list(x)\n\n    print(ls([1]), ls(range(2)))\n    print(ls(x for x in [4, 5]))\n\nCPython prints `[1] [0, 1]` then `[4, 5]`. zypy (dea6d08b and later)\nprints `[1] [0, 1]`, then `[]`, then `TypeError: 'NoneType' object is\nnot iterable`. With only generator callers, `ls(x for x in [4, 5])`\nis correct. The same happens for `array('B', x)` in the callee.","files":null}]}